Tsar Nicholas ii gave this beautiful egg  to his consort 100 years ago to mark the  300th anniversary of the Romanov dynasty  the Faberge egg is part of the Romanov  horde the treasures and symbols of a  family that made Russia great and  influenced European history for  centuries  Paul Edward kulakowski is a descendant  of the dynasty a great-great grandson of  Tsar Alexander the third oh I feel a lot  of pride I'm very happy to see this it's  amazing to see all these beautiful  artifacts not only are they beautiful in  the sense of artwork but also the the  spirituality of these items the emotion  that they gave me and of course they're  related to to my family so for me sort  of family history so for me it's a great  joy just to walk through here Moscow is  the city where the Tsar's were crowned  but the Romanovs history started in  kostroma  several hundred kilometers to the north  Yevgeniy culligan organizes a festival  every year here he's recounting the  story for some Swiss visitors it was in  the impassive monastery that Mikhail  feodorovich was elected as the first  Roman Afsar at the time Russia was being  torn apart by Civil War and foreign  invaders the Romanovs used cunning skill  and brute force to make Russia stable  and powerful that ascent began in Costa  Roma so you get a good sense of how much  happened here you can feel this long  family history this long dynasty that a  lot of important decisions were made  here cos drama thrived as the young  Russian Empire expanded the city became  one of its most important centres of  commercial and cultural activity but  Kostroma is not just about the past  Yevgeniy culligan and a team of friends  set up an art project called stanza  meaning station in a former factory  once a year he invites artists from  across Europe to take part in a dance  festival  dinasty Romano Catherine the grade and  Peter the Great and the other Romanovs  always had an open european view of  everything new and progressive modern  art is always progressive I think the  Czar's would have liked what we're doing  here today the Czar's heritage plays  only a minor role in Costa Roma and  Moscow is paying little attention to the  Romanov anniversary  but the treasures of the Kremlin's speak  for themselves and there's still a lot  of important artifacts in the diamond  fund which of course is also in the  Moscow Kremlin but they are not really  part of this exhibition so actually  there is a fourth place here in the  Moscow Kremlin that you have to go to  even though it's not officially part of  the exhibition it would have been nice  to see all the crowns just lying next to  each other so you could see them sort of  in a quick sequence but Paul kulakowski  is just glad that so many czars treasure  survived the Russian Revolution and the  Soviet era  they'll be on display at the Kremlin  until the end of January
 

